# 13 - Complex Data Types (Array, Map, Struct) (PySpark Practical)

This practical covers:
- ArrayType
- MapType
- StructType nested
- explode(), explode_outer()
- posexplode()
- getItem()
- JSON flattening techniques
- nested JSON flattening

Complex datatypes are very common in real projects (API data).

---

## 0) Setup

```python
from pyspark.sql import SparkSession
from pyspark.sql.functions import (
    col, explode, explode_outer, posexplode,
    split, array_contains
)
from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, IntegerType, ArrayType, MapType

spark = SparkSession.builder     .appName("ComplexDataTypesPractical")     .master("local[*]")     .getOrCreate()
```

---

## 1) ArrayType Practical

### 1.1 Create DataFrame with array

```python
data = [
    (1, "Pramod", ["Python", "SQL", "PySpark"]),
    (2, "Akash", ["Excel", "HR"]),
    (3, "Rahul", None)
]

schema = StructType([
    StructField("id", IntegerType(), True),
    StructField("name", StringType(), True),
    StructField("skills", ArrayType(StringType()), True)
])

df = spark.createDataFrame(data, schema)
df.show(truncate=False)
df.printSchema()
```

---

### 1.2 explode() (Most Used)

```python
df_explode = df.select("id", "name", explode("skills").alias("skill"))
df_explode.show(truncate=False)
```

⚠️ explode() removes rows where array is null.

---

### 1.3 explode_outer()

```python
df_explode_outer = df.select("id", "name", explode_outer("skills").alias("skill"))
df_explode_outer.show(truncate=False)
```

explode_outer keeps null rows.

---

### 1.4 array_contains()

```python
df.filter(array_contains(col("skills"), "SQL")).show(truncate=False)
```

---

## 2) posexplode()

posexplode gives:
- position index
- value

```python
df_pos = df.select("id", "name", posexplode("skills").alias("pos", "skill"))
df_pos.show(truncate=False)
```

